# Target C-II — low-energy dominant O--O pairs from distinct phases must cross

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only structural theorem  
**Scope:** full-core Type-II near-disk branch, dominant exact `pi/3` orbit, retained outer-to-outer pair weights  
**Dependencies:** audited Type-II near-disk perimeter geometry; audited Section 157 outer-to-outer train bracing; audited convex-pocket cyclic-order transfer; audited train-crossing graph  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The current dominant O--O staircase produces linear retained pair weight in one exact
three-phase train orbit, but by itself does not say how those trains interact across
phase classes.  The missing bridge is geometric: low terminal directional energy pins
an O--O train close to the diameter normal to its contact phase.  Two such near-diametral
trains from different exact phase classes have alternating outer endpoints, hence must
cross.

This yields a direct lower bound on the phase-star crossing mass from retained O--O
pair weights and their terminal energies.

Let the three exact projective phase classes of the dominant orbit be indexed by
`i=1,2,3`.  For phase `i`, define

- `P_i` = normalized retained dominant O--O pair weight;
- `E_i` = normalized retained-pair-weighted terminal directional energy;
- `X_ij` = normalized strict-rhombus crossing mass between phase classes `i,j`.

Then for every `i!=j`,

\[
\boxed{
X_{ij}
\ge
\left(P_i-{2E_i\over2-\sqrt3}\right)_+
\left(P_j-{2E_j\over2-\sqrt3}\right)_+.
}
\tag{LE.1}

More generally, for every positive `lambda_i,lambda_j` satisfying

\[
\lambda_i+\lambda_j<2-\sqrt3,
\]

\[
\boxed{
X_{ij}
\ge
\left(P_i-{E_i\over\lambda_i}\right)_+
\left(P_j-{E_j\over\lambda_j}\right)_+.
}
\tag{LE.2}

Equation (LE.1) is the limit of (LE.2) as both thresholds increase to
`(2-sqrt(3))/2`.

This is the first direct inequality in the current stack that converts retained
boundary O--O structure into a **lower** bound on an internal phase-star crossing
population.

---

## 1. Retained pair weights and pair energy

Freeze disjoint shrinking phase windows around the three atoms of the dominant exact
orbit.  For a dominant O--O train `tau`, let

\[
a_\tau,b_\tau\in[0,1]
\]

be the retained weights of its two selected outer terminal occurrences and put

\[
w_\tau:=\min(a_\tau,b_\tau).
\tag{LE.3}

For one exact phase class `i`,

\[
P_i
:={1\over D}\sum_{\tau\in i}w_\tau.
\tag{LE.4}

Let the exact route directional-energy contributions of the two terminal occurrences
be `e_{tau,1},e_{tau,2}>=0`, and set

\[
e_\tau:=e_{\tau,1}+e_{\tau,2}.
\]

Define

\[
E_i
:={1\over D}\sum_{\tau\in i}w_\tau e_\tau.
\tag{LE.5}

Because `w_tau<=a_tau,b_tau`,

\[
w_\tau(e_{\tau,1}+e_{\tau,2})
\le a_\tau e_{\tau,1}+b_\tau e_{\tau,2}.
\]

Therefore the pair-weighted phase energies are genuinely paid by the already selected
dominant outer-edge energy:

\[
\boxed{
E_1+E_2+E_3\le E_{dom}\le\delta+o(1).
}
\tag{LE.6}

No new energy currency is introduced.

---

## 2. One O--O train: terminal energy measures deviation from an ideal diameter

Take one outer-to-outer complete train `tau` with projective contact phase `phi`.  Let
its two outer terminal positions cut the round-limit boundary into a smaller oriented
normal-angle interval of width

\[
0\le w\le\pi.
\]

Audited Section 157 gives actual diameter crossings with both transverse signs.  In the
Type-II near-disk limit this forces one representative of the train phase into that
normal interval.  Put the interval at `[0,w]` and write that phase representative as

\[
\phi\in[0,w].
\]

The two endpoint tangent directions are projectively `pi/2` and `w+pi/2`.  Therefore
Section 157's terminal-energy calculation is exactly

\[
e_\tau
\ge
[1-\sin\phi]+[1-\sin(w-\phi)]-o(1).
\tag{LE.7}

Define endpoint deviations from the ideal phase-normal diameter by

\[
\alpha:=\left|\phi-{\pi\over2}\right|,
\qquad
\beta:=\left|w-\phi-{\pi\over2}\right|.
\tag{LE.8}

Then

\[
1-\sin\phi=1-\cos\alpha,
\qquad
1-\sin(w-\phi)=1-\cos\beta,
\]

so

\[
\boxed{
e_\tau
\ge(1-\cos\alpha)+(1-\cos\beta)-o(1).}
\tag{LE.9}

Geometrically, after orienting the two outer endpoints oppositely around the round
limit, their normal angles differ from the ideal antipodal normals

\[
\phi-{\pi\over2},
\qquad
\phi+{\pi\over2}
\]

by at most `alpha` and `beta` respectively.

The `o(1)` is uniform after freezing the phase windows and taking the Type-II near-disk
limit.

---

## 3. Low-energy O--O endpoints lie on opposite normal branches

For the cross-phase argument we must exclude the possibility that both terminal
positions use the same oriented representative of the projective tangent direction.
This is automatic at the energy scale used below.

Put

\[
\Lambda:={2-\sqrt3\over2}
=1-{\sqrt3\over2}
<0.134.
\tag{LE.10}

Suppose `e_tau<=Lambda-o(1)`.  By convexity of `1-cos x` on `[0,pi/2]`, equation
(LE.9) gives

\[
2\left[1-\cos\left({\alpha+\beta\over2}\right)\right]
\le\Lambda+o(1).
\]

Thus `alpha+beta<0.74` for all sufficiently large sources.  If both outer terminals
used the same oriented normal branch, their circular normal separation would be at
most `alpha+beta+o(1)`, so their Euclidean separation in a diameter-`D` near-disk would
be below `0.37D+o(D)`.

But Section 157's O--O bracing estimate gives, uniformly in the present live window
`t<=1.860`,

\[
{r_\tau\over D}
\ge1-\delta-{e_\tau\over2}-o(1).
\tag{LE.11}

The matched `g=0.0076` envelope gives `delta<0.088` for `t<=1.860`, while
`e_tau<=Lambda<0.134`; hence the right side of (LE.11) exceeds `0.845-o(1)`.
The same-branch alternative is impossible.

Therefore every train at the threshold (LE.10) has one terminal near each of the two
antipodal ideal normals of its phase.

---

## 4. Two different phases: failure of endpoint alternation costs `2-sqrt(3)`

Take two O--O trains `tau,sigma` from two distinct exact atoms of the same dominant
orbit.  Their projective phase directions differ by exactly

\[
{\pi\over3}.
\]

Use the opposite-branch orientation from Section 3.  In the perfect zero-energy limit,
the four endpoint normals are the endpoints of two diameters whose axes differ by
`pi/3`; hence they alternate on the circle.

Suppose the actual four outer endpoints fail to alternate.  Then at least one of the
two ideal `pi/3` boundary gaps must close.  Consequently two endpoint deviations, one
from `tau` and one from `sigma`, have sum at least

\[
{\pi\over3}-o(1).
\]

If those deviations are `a,b`, convexity gives

\[
(1-\cos a)+(1-\cos b)
\ge
2\left(1-\cos{a+b\over2}\right)
\ge
2-\sqrt3-o(1).
\tag{LE.12}

Those two endpoint costs are included in `e_tau+e_sigma`.  Therefore

\[
\boxed{
\text{nonalternating endpoints}
\Longrightarrow
e_\tau+e_\sigma\ge2-\sqrt3-o(1).
}
\tag{LE.13}

Equivalently, for any fixed thresholds satisfying

\[
\lambda_i+\lambda_j<2-\sqrt3,
\]

every phase-`i` O--O train with `e_tau<=lambda_i` and every phase-`j` O--O train with
`e_sigma<=lambda_j` have alternating endpoints for all sufficiently large sources.

---

## 5. Alternating endpoints force a strict-rhombus train crossing

The audited convex-pocket augmentation pushes outer train endpoints to the loaded-hull
boundary while preserving cyclic order.  The corresponding complete-train midpoint
curves are Jordan arcs.  Two such O--O arcs with alternating endpoints on a convex
boundary cannot be disjoint.

Within the audited complete-train system, two distinct trains meet only through a
strict-rhombus crossing.  Hence the low-energy phase-`i` and phase-`j` families from
Section 4 form a complete bipartite subgraph of the strict-rhombus train crossing
graph.

This uses only topological endpoint alternation plus the already-audited train system;
it does not assume straight train paths.

---

## 6. Weighted Markov extraction gives the crossing lower bound

For phase `i`, let

\[
P_i(\lambda_i)
:={1\over D}
\sum_{\substack{\tau\in i\\e_\tau\le\lambda_i}}w_\tau.
\]

Weighted Markov gives

\[
\boxed{
P_i(\lambda_i)
\ge
\left(P_i-{E_i\over\lambda_i}\right)_+.
}
\tag{LE.14}

The same holds for phase `j`.

Every train in the two extracted families crosses every train in the other family.
Since all retained pair weights lie in `[0,1]`, the actual normalized crossing count
between the two phase classes dominates the product of their total retained weights:

\[
X_{ij}
\ge P_i(\lambda_i)P_j(\lambda_j).
\]

Combining with (LE.14) proves (LE.2).

Finally choose

\[
\lambda_i=\lambda_j
={2-\sqrt3\over2}-\varepsilon,
\]

pass first to the source limit and phase-window limit, then let `epsilon downarrow0`.
This gives (LE.1).

---

## 7. Why this matters for the current frontier

The dominant O--O staircase gives, for example,

\[
t\le1.844
\Longrightarrow
P_1+P_2+P_3>0.29-o(1),
\]

while the total pair energy obeys

\[
E_1+E_2+E_3\le\delta\approx0.05.
\]

Equation (LE.1) means this boundary data cannot be assigned among the three exact phase
classes independently of the internal phase-star graph.  Whenever two phase classes
retain low-energy O--O mass, a definite block of their crossing matrix is forced to be
present.  Triangle-freeness then requires compensating missing crossings to the third
phase through the same center/leaf correlation used in the hostile-audited `g=0.0076`
capacity theorem.

A preliminary numerical optimization of this coupled system at the coarse `t<=1.844`
endpoint still leaves about `0.0033D^2` crossing-capacity slack, so (LE.1) alone does
not close that entire interval.  Nearer the live `1.84355` frontier the slack drops to
about `6e-5D^2` under the same coarse relaxation, indicating that the new bridge is
quantitatively relevant.

Those numerical values are exploratory only and are not theorem claims.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only structural bridge from boundary O--O pairs to
internal phase-star crossings. C-II and Level I remain open.
